WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:03.429
INTRO:
Nowadays, WhatsApp has become a meme on YouTube

00:00:03.429 --> 00:00:07.900
due to all of the bitcoin and crypto scammers
in the comment section that tell you to contact

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:09.680
them through WhatsApp.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:14.519
Given this poor association and the relative
unpopularity of WhatsApp within the US, a

00:00:14.519 --> 00:00:18.600
lot of Americans likely write off the app
as just a gateway for scammers.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.890
But, outside the US, WhatsApp is a lot more
than just that.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:28.180
In fact, in many developing countries and
Europe, WhatsApp is often the primary form

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:29.710
of communication.

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:34.880
Instead of using traditional SMS and calls,
WhatsApp uses the internet to facilitate these

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:39.680
communications which is extremely convenient
and cheap, especially for international communication.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:45.379
In other words, WhatsApp is basically the
iMessage and Facetime of non-Americans.

00:00:45.379 --> 00:00:49.200
Considering this, I don’t think you’d
be surprised to hear that WhatsApp is the

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:54.160
world’s most popular messaging app with
a total of 2 billion active monthly users.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.400
Despite WhatsApp’s explosive success, both
of WhatsApp’s founders left the company

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.630
in 2018 citing privacy and monetization concerns.

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:07.770
So, here’s what happened to the founders
of the most popular messaging app in the world.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:13.390
JAN KOUM:
Taking a look back, the story of WhatsApp

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:17.400
circles back to two men named Jan Koum and
Brian Acton.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:22.399
Jan was born on February 24, 1976, in Kyiv,
Ukraine to a Jewish family.

00:01:22.399 --> 00:01:27.009
Jan was an only child, so his parents gave
him extra love and attention, but no amount

00:01:27.009 --> 00:01:29.969
of care was able to cover up the reality of
their living situation.

00:01:29.969 --> 00:01:35.310
Jan’s house didn’t even have running water,
and it wasn’t much better at school.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:39.030
Jan says that his school didn’t have a bathroom
inside the building.

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:43.209
So kids had to run across the parking lot
to just use the bathroom which was especially

00:01:43.209 --> 00:01:46.810
painful when the temperature was -20 degrees
Celsius.

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:50.989
Aside from poor living conditions, Jan says
that the social life wasn’t that much better

00:01:50.989 --> 00:01:53.499
due to all the discrimination against Jews.

00:01:53.499 --> 00:01:58.779
Jan’s family had put up with this for decades,
but when Jan turned 16, they decided to immigrate

00:01:58.779 --> 00:02:00.770
to Mountain View, California.

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:04.479
This move eliminated a lot of the outright
discrimination they experienced and the lack

00:02:04.479 --> 00:02:06.600
of opportunities that accompanied socialism.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:11.640
But, life in the US was still quite difficult
because they had no money and had trouble

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:12.640
communicating.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.689
To make ends meet, Jan’s mother had to start
babysitting and Jan had to become a cleaner

00:02:16.689 --> 00:02:18.200
at a grocery store.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.030
The family also had to use welfare checks
and food stamps.

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:25.790
In terms of school, Jan admits that he wasn’t
the best of students and that he barely got

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:29.540
through high school, but this didn’t mean
that he hated learning.

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:32.930
He enjoyed reading books about computer science
and teaching himself coding.

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:37.460
Apparently, he would buy coding books from
a local book store, read them really fast,

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:39.330
and then return them to save money.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:44.209
Anyway, moving onto college, Jan would attend
San Jose State University and he would pick

00:02:44.209 --> 00:02:48.540
up a side job as a security tester at Ernst
and Young to pay for his education.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:53.230
But, this job would end up being a lot more
valuable than just helping pay for college.

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:58.020
You see, Jan would meet an early Yahoo employee
named Brain Acton while completing a project

00:02:58.020 --> 00:02:59.170
for Ernst &amp; Young.

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:03.709
6 months later, Brian would help Jan get a
job at Yahoo, and that marked the beginning

00:03:03.709 --> 00:03:07.410
of the partnership that would eventually turn
into WhatsApp.

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:12.510
BRIAN ACTON:
Taking a look back at Brian’s past, it’s

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:14.950
a lot different than Jan’s past.

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:18.760
Brian was born on February 17, 1972, in Michigan.

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:22.390
His mother ran a freight shipping business,
and she was the one that really inspired Brian

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:24.319
to pursue entrepreneurship.

00:03:24.319 --> 00:03:29.270
Ironically, Jan actually hates this word and
doesn’t like it when people call him an

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:30.270
entrepreneur.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:33.659
He feels that the main goal of traditional
entrepreneurs is to make money while his main

00:03:33.659 --> 00:03:35.709
goal is to make useful products.

00:03:35.709 --> 00:03:40.319
Anyway, going back to Brian, it looks like
he was a star student at school.

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:43.980
He would attend Lake Howell High School in
central Florida and would earn a full scholarship

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:46.959
to study engineering at the University of
Pennsylvania.

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:50.500
He only stuck around at UPenn for a year though
before he switched over to Stanford to get

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:52.060
a computer science degree.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:55.750
With such a rich educational background, it
was easy for Brian to enter the workforce

00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:59.700
and switch from one job to another until he
found his calling.

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:04.340
In 1992, he started off as a systems administrator
at Rockwell International.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:08.090
After that, he became a product tester at
Apple before becoming a product tester at

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:09.260
Adobe.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:13.160
While Brian didn’t know exactly what he
wanted to do in the early days, he soon found

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:16.699
himself looking back at his childhood and
his mother and decided that he wanted to give

00:04:16.699 --> 00:04:18.609
entrepreneurship a shot.

00:04:18.609 --> 00:04:23.789
So, in 1996, he joined Yahoo as their 44th
employee and this is when he would meet Jan

00:04:23.789 --> 00:04:24.789
Koum.

00:04:24.789 --> 00:04:29.300
For the next decade, Brian and Jan would stick
around at Yahoo, and just consistently save

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:31.170
up their money.

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:34.889
While their professional lives were progressing
pretty smoothly, the same could not be said

00:04:34.889 --> 00:04:36.500
about their personal lives.

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:41.419
In February of 1996, Jan would verbally and
physically threaten an ex-girlfriend after

00:04:41.419 --> 00:04:45.910
they broke up, and the girlfriend would end
up filing a restraining order against him.

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:49.210
Jan has since expressed that he’s extremely
embarrassed and ashamed of his behavior.

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:54.130
In the meantime, Brian was having a great
time in the late 1990s.

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:57.630
He was investing heavily in dot-com companies
which made him a multi-millionaire.

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:01.550
But, once the dot-com bubble burst, he would
lose most of it.

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:06.379
While I’m sure he was fine financially thanks
to his job and savings, this was likely an

00:05:06.379 --> 00:05:08.180
extremely painful event.

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:13.020
Nonetheless, the duo got through these events
and would eventually leave Yahoo in 2007.

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:17.330
Soon after, they both tried to get jobs at
Facebook, but both of them would be rejected.

00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:21.300
I don’t think Jan nor Brian really cared
that much though.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:25.220
With over a decade of experience within the
tech field, they had already made plenty of

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:26.220
money.

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:30.300
So, they decided to take it easy and go on
a one-year vacation to South America and play

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:31.300
ultimate frisbee.

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:36.410
When they came back, Jan would buy an iPhone
in January of 2009, and this was the beginning

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:37.780
of WhatsApp.

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:44.350
FOUNDING WHATSAPP:
As soon as Jan got an iPhone, he knew that

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:46.259
the App store was going to be huge.

00:05:46.259 --> 00:05:50.349
So, he started talking with Brian and another
friend named Alex Fishman about potential

00:05:50.349 --> 00:05:52.169
apps that they could launch.

00:05:52.169 --> 00:05:56.770
One month later, on Jan’s 33rd birthday,
he would incorporate the company WhatsApp.

00:05:56.770 --> 00:06:00.760
The name is of course a reference to the popular
saying What’s Up.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:05.099
Despite incorporating the company, Jan didn’t
really know what they wanted to create.

00:06:05.099 --> 00:06:09.379
He knew they wanted to do something relating
to social media, hence the name, but initially,

00:06:09.379 --> 00:06:11.530
he was actually leaning towards status updates.

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:17.020
It wasn’t until August of 2009 that he incorporated
a messaging aspect into the app, and this

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:19.590
is what really accelerated the adoption of
the app.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:24.580
At this point, Jan did most of the work and
owned the entirety of the company.

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:28.599
In the meantime, Brian was still unemployed
and looking for potential employment.

00:06:28.599 --> 00:06:33.110
So, Jan offered to make Brian a cofounder
if he could raise the company some money.

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:35.880
Fortunately for Brian, this was rather easy.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:40.250
He hit up a couple of his friends from Yahoo
and was able to raise a total of $250,000

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:41.250
for WhatsApp.

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:44.880
And with that, Brian and Jan were able to
go all-in on WhatsApp.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:48.860
From the very beginning, one of the duo’s
top priorities was privacy.

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:52.629
Jan says quote, “We want to know as little
about our users as possible...

00:06:52.629 --> 00:06:56.580
We're not advertisement-driven so we don't
need personal databases."

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:59.629
You could say these were some famous last
words.

00:06:59.629 --> 00:07:04.470
Anyway, WhatsApp quickly started to gain popularity,
especially within developing countries where

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:08.199
WhatsApp was the best internet-based mobile
messaging service available.

00:07:08.199 --> 00:07:12.139
Hundreds of thousands of users turned into
millions of users and millions of users turned

00:07:12.139 --> 00:07:14.150
into tens of millions of users.

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:17.819
Despite the popularity of the app, the duo
didn’t really have a smart way to monetize

00:07:17.819 --> 00:07:18.819
the app.

00:07:18.819 --> 00:07:22.080
So, they were burning money left right trying
to keep operations afloat.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:27.710
Fortunately, in 2011, they would successfully
raise $8 million from Sequoia Capital, but

00:07:27.710 --> 00:07:31.870
both of them knew that with how fast WhatsApp
was growing, it was just a matter of time

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:33.930
until this money would run out.

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:37.060
Thankfully, Mark would reach out just in time.

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:42.620
HITTING IT BIG:
WhatsApp caught the attention of Mark Zuckerberg

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:47.350
very early on, and Mark would quickly start
building up a relationship with Jan and Brian.

00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:52.710
In 2012, Mark called Jan and the two would
get some coffee and go on a hike.

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:57.629
Over the next two years, the two would stay
in contact, and eventually, in February of

00:07:57.629 --> 00:08:01.819
2014, Mark would invite Jan over for dinner,
and during dinner, Mark made Jan an offer

00:08:01.819 --> 00:08:06.080
he couldn’t refuse.

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:11.460
He offered to buy the money-losing company
with little revenue for a whopping $19 billion.

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:16.349
WhatsApp was definitely not worth anywhere
near $19 billion at the time, but Mark could

00:08:16.349 --> 00:08:19.050
see the long-term potential of the company.

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:23.349
Meanwhile, Jan likely knew that WhatsApp could
eventually grow well past $19 billion naturally.

00:08:23.349 --> 00:08:28.170
But, that would be a long and tough journey
filled with risk, so, taking the buyout was

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:29.389
a no-brainer.

00:08:29.389 --> 00:08:33.970
Soon after, Jan would sign the acquisition
papers on the door of his old welfare office,

00:08:33.970 --> 00:08:37.340
and with that Jan and Brian were officially
billionaires.

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:41.290
With the backing of Facebook, Jan and Brian
were able to test and implement new features

00:08:41.290 --> 00:08:43.420
faster than ever before.

00:08:43.420 --> 00:08:47.100
One of the first features they implemented
after the acquisition was voice calls.

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:51.410
And by June of 2016, more than 100 million
voice calls were being placed on WhatsApp

00:08:51.410 --> 00:08:52.740
on a daily basis.

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:57.840
Soon after they launched video calls, two-step
verification, and WhatsApp Business.

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:01.580
Aside from introducing new features, WhatsApp
could relax their monetization effort thanks

00:09:01.580 --> 00:09:02.580
to Facebook.

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:07.830
So, in January of 2016, they would remove
their annual $1 fee which made WhatsApp more

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:10.220
accessible than ever before.

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:14.890
Despite all these advantages, there were also
a few notable disadvantages namely a reduction

00:09:14.890 --> 00:09:16.560
in user privacy.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:20.830
During the summer of 2016, WhatsApp would
begin sharing user info with Facebook for

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:22.130
targeted ads.

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:26.760
Neither Brian nor Jan were big fans of this
decision, and they would both leave Facebook

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:32.210
soon after, but not before securing their
full stock compensations.

00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:35.980
SIGNAL:
Brian would be the first to leave Facebook

00:09:35.980 --> 00:09:41.960
in September of 2017, and Jan would leave
Facebook 7 months later in April of 2018.

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:46.070
Ironically, both of them would start trashing
Facebook as soon as they left.

00:09:46.070 --> 00:09:50.170
Jan has expressed his grievances about Facebook
to the media on several occasions.

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:53.960
Meanwhile, Brian straight up advocates for
people to delete Facebook.

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:56.550
Clearly, money hasn’t bought their loyalty.

00:09:56.550 --> 00:10:01.410
But anyway, Jan has more or less just retired
ever since he left Facebook, and we don’t

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:03.200
really hear from him that much.

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:07.120
The only time his name comes up in the media
nowadays is whenever he makes another large

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:08.120
donation.

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:13.240
One of his largest donations was $556 million
to the Silicon Valley Community Foundation.

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:16.860
Jan says that Facebook barely changed his
life and that he still lives in the same house,

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:20.420
hangs out with the same friends, and more
or less does the same things.

00:10:20.420 --> 00:10:22.900
He just has a lot more money in his bank account.

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:27.860
Brian, on the other hand, is more active than
ever before with the Signal Foundation.

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:31.630
One of the first things Brian did after leaving
Facebook was founding a competing messaging

00:10:31.630 --> 00:10:33.390
platform called Signal.

00:10:33.390 --> 00:10:37.740
Signal is a non-profit messaging platform
that focuses on collecting as little data

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:39.230
as possible.

00:10:39.230 --> 00:10:43.560
Everything on the app is encrypted and so
far Brian has funded it with $100 million

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:44.850
of his own money.

00:10:44.850 --> 00:10:49.540
Signal has actually become quite popular growing
an active user base of 40 million users as

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:51.450
of the start of 2021.

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:55.730
And given the virtuous goals of Signal and
how much funding they have, I can’t see

00:10:55.730 --> 00:10:59.250
why the app won’t just become even more
popular within the coming years.

00:10:59.250 --> 00:11:03.500
Eventually, they may even be able to give
Telegram and WhatsApp a run for their money

00:11:03.500 --> 00:11:04.860
which is the goal.

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:10.300
So, Brian may end up being known for founding,
not one but two game-changing messaging platforms,

00:11:10.300 --> 00:11:12.510
one for himself and one for the people.

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:15.040
Do you guys think Signal can replace WhatsApp?

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:16.040
Comment that down below.

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:20.050
Also, drop a like if you’re glad that Brian
is standing up to Facebook.

00:11:20.050 --> 00:11:24.170
And of course, consider checking out our international
channels to watch our videos in other languages

00:11:24.170 --> 00:11:30.130
and consider subscribing to see more questions
logically answered.

